knowledge:
- key_parameters:
    - pattern=A regular expression used in find_nodes() to search through the name attribute of nodes (ignores capitalization). ;;
    - semtypes=A character vector of 4-letter semantic type codes (e.g., "dsyn", "gngm") used in find_nodes() to filter nodes by category. ;;
    - names=A character vector used in find_nodes() to select nodes by exact name (ignores capitalization). ;;
    - match=A logical value in find_nodes(); set to FALSE to prune/exclude matching nodes from the results. ;;
    - weights=A numeric vector of edge weights passed to find_paths() to guide the shortest path search. ;;
    - node_semtypes_out / node_names_out / edge_preds_out=Character vectors in make_edge_weights() specifying features to discourage by assigning them the maximum possible weight. ;;
    - node_semtypes_in / node_names_in / edge_preds_in=Character vectors in make_edge_weights() specifying features to encourage by assigning them lower weights. ;;
    - collapse=A logical value in get_middle_nodes(); if FALSE, returns middle nodes for each source-target pair in a tibble instead of a single collapsed vector. ;;
    - is_path=A logical value in summarize_semtypes(); set to FALSE when summarizing a single grown node set instead of paths.
- alternatives:
    - SQL dump files=The raw SemMedDB PREDICATION table can be downloaded directly from the National Library of Medicine and processed using scripts in the package's inst/script folder to generate custom graph representations.
- when_to_use:
    - Use this package when you want to programmatically explore literature-derived connections, paths, and relationships between biological concepts (e.g., diseases, genes, drugs) annotated in SemMedDB.
- pitfalls:
    - SemMedDB is computationally-derived, meaning some semantic types or predications may be noisy or nonsensical. ;;
    - Manual operations on edge attributes can be extremely slow due to the massive size of the graph (over 18 million edges). Node operations are faster but still involve roughly 290,000 nodes. ;;
    - Unweighted shortest path algorithms often return uninformative paths through high-degree hub nodes (e.g., "Infant", "Child", "Woman") via generic predicates like "PROCESS_OF". Users must actively use edge reweighting or middle-node exclusion to find biologically meaningful paths.
